+
+On 2026-11-19 I removed a small white ▶ mark from the door plate with cv2 inpainting; the user objected: "你不要去这样改啊 然后你让 GPT 去生图啊". Any change to the content of an art asset — even erasing a tiny label or seam — is to be done by GPT: I prepare the input image and a prompt, the user runs it and sends the result back.
+
+Later the same day I drew the shutter's iris blades in SwiftUI; the user said: "你也别自己拿程序生…你去研究逻辑，然后让 GPT 去生素材，这样好看一些". So visible parts that move are also GPT art: I work out the mechanism and geometry, give GPT a silhouette template with known pivot/centre, and animate the sprite it returns.
+
+On 2026-11-21, after I built the fold-open film library in plain SwiftUI cards and buttons, the user set a standing rule: "所有新的界面不要自己用程序画 你来出创意 gpt 作图 要求拟物化 尽量不要像网页或者菜单 这条记住". Every new screen (the film library, the waist-level finder, any future page) starts as my creative concept of a real physical object — a film drawer, a light box with a contact sheet, a camera hood — drawn by GPT through /codex-image; code only places live content (photos, text, viewfinder) into the measured rects of that art and handles touch. Flat card lists, pill buttons and menu rows are what to avoid.
+
+**Why:** my retouches leave smudges and my code-drawn parts look plain; the user wants the look to come from the image model (same rule as [[cameragr-working-preferences]]).
+**How to apply:** For any new screen: write the concept (what object it is, materials, where each live element sits), generate it with GPT, inspect it, then fit live content into it; show the user the concept and art before wiring. Existing code-drawn pages (store, settings, detail) predate the rule — ask before redoing them. I may crop, align, cut out alpha, composite GPT output, measure rects, and write the animation/geometry. I may not inpaint/clone/paint over pixels, and should not ship code-drawn stand-ins for visible parts when a GPT sprite can do it — offer the template + prompt instead. If GPT output has a stray mark or wrong text, report it and give a follow-up prompt. (My interpretation: light compositing effects in code such as a shadow over a window are tolerated, but ask if in doubt.)
